{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790680819,"nonce":"2z2kopuT8MQbUO9yE8NEQsimhYM=","title":"Make rayzor-gpu the complete xgpu adapter without losing Rayzor compute","message":"Rayzor's existing `gpu` crate and `rayzor-gpu.rpkg` should implement xgpu's canonical GPU API rather than introducing a second package. Generate the `rayzor.gpu` extern surface from xgpu's API/IDL and package it beside the native library.\n\nRayzor owns the features above the shared object model: `@:shader` Haxe-to-WGSL lowering, lazy tensor graphs, fusion, quantized kernels, and Metal/CUDA compute policy. These must remain extensions in the same crate. The wgpu path should use xgpu's extension seam so generated rendering/compute calls and Rayzor compute share device, queue, and `GpuBuffer` handles.\n\nThe native adapter must use Rayzor's plugin ABI for strings, bytes, errors, objects, method descriptors, and package exports. Add an externally completable pending/resolve/reject host ABI to `rayzor.concurrent.Future\u003cT\u003e` so wgpu callbacks can fulfill generated Promise operations without storing a Haxe closure or linking another runtime into the plugin.\n\nAcceptance:\n\n- the complete generated xgpu API is available under `rayzor.gpu`;\n- existing `@:shader` examples compile through the generated shader/device API;\n- lazy and specialized compute remains Rayzor-owned and works over the shared wgpu resources;\n- generated externs and every native library are shipped together in `rayzor-gpu.rpkg`;\n- no bare extern artifact or parallel GPU package is released;\n- native and browser package paths retain Rayzor's existing extension behavior.\n\nCross-repository tracking: xgpu issue a28249d95f9b3c97141b7375136ad811b30b3d14f55166b3df6c5af925439d92.","files":null}]}